1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
It could be a 2,000-mile border or a nuclear waste site.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:11,000
It would be a very bad idea for somebody to attempt to break in here.

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:15,000
It could be a padlock, a biometric lock,

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:17,000
identification completed.

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:20,000
or a bank vault.

6
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:24,000
It could be a concrete barrier, a steel cage,

7
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:27,000
or bulletproof plexiglass.

8
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:29,900
They're all designed with one intent.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,840
Whether by land, air, or sea, the perimeter is secure.

10
00:00:40,940 --> 00:00:44,660
Now it's Keep Out on Modern Marvels.

11
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,500
Every day we see these signs.

12
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:02,500
Keep out.

13
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:05,640
They protect something secretive or valuable.

14
00:01:05,740 --> 00:01:09,940
Keep us from harm or restrict access.

15
00:01:10,040 --> 00:01:12,140
Signs are only a warning.

16
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:16,660
Behind them is the real business of keeping people out.

17
00:01:16,760 --> 00:01:19,180
Modern safes have been keeping thieves out of valuables

18
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:20,660
for more than a century.

19
00:01:20,760 --> 00:01:23,100
Most rely on combination locks.

20
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:25,400
But what happens if you lose that combination

21
00:01:25,401 --> 00:01:27,940
combination, and you're the one that's being kept out of your

22
00:01:28,040 --> 00:01:29,200
own safe.

23
00:01:29,300 --> 00:01:31,800
That's where eight-time world champion safecracker Jeff

24
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:33,700
Sitar comes in.

25
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:35,940
They open up safes and vaults for a living.

26
00:01:36,040 --> 00:01:38,440
And what I specialize in is what they call the art of

27
00:01:38,540 --> 00:01:42,860
manipulation, opening safes with just my hands, not drilling.

28
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:45,860
Sitar uses sight, hearing, and feel to open anything from

29
00:01:45,960 --> 00:01:48,660
home safes to bank vaults.

30
00:01:48,760 --> 00:01:50,760
I've opened up a lot of bank vaults.

31
00:01:50,860 --> 00:01:53,000
My daily routine is cracking safes,

32
00:01:53,100 --> 00:01:54,900
and I've been doing this for 30 years.

33
00:01:54,901 --> 00:01:57,940
I practice 9 to 5, 5 to 6 days a week.

34
00:01:58,340 --> 00:01:59,840
This is what I do all day long.

35
00:02:00,540 --> 00:02:03,200
Other than his senses, the only tools Sitar uses

36
00:02:03,201 --> 00:02:05,180
are a magnifying glass to view the dial

37
00:02:05,181 --> 00:02:07,020
and an audio amplifier.

38
00:02:10,320 --> 00:02:13,780
We challenged Sitar to try to open a 60-ton bank vault

39
00:02:13,781 --> 00:02:16,980
in New Jersey that has a possible 100 million combinations.

40
00:02:17,820 --> 00:02:21,200
What we're standing in front of is a really beefed-up bank vault.

41
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:23,660
It's a Remington Sherman, and it's probably

42
00:02:23,661 --> 00:02:26,160
from the 1930s.

43
00:02:26,260 --> 00:02:28,660
I mean, I don't know which number I'm going to get first.

44
00:02:28,760 --> 00:02:30,460
Each one reads differently.

45
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:33,500
So I got to find out the personality of the lock,

46
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:35,800
and then a plan of attack to open it.

47
00:02:35,801 --> 00:02:37,800
He's got a lot of money.

48
00:02:51,820 --> 00:02:53,820
Sitar knows how to attack any lock

49
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:55,820
because he's spent years practicing.

50
00:02:59,820 --> 00:03:02,700
He keeps a collection of locks he's cracked in his house.

51
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:05,460
I leave these locks around to brush up something,

52
00:03:05,461 --> 00:03:07,660
or maybe I have a new idea on how to open

53
00:03:07,760 --> 00:03:09,700
or manipulate a safe open.

54
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:12,160
Even with a key for extra security,

55
00:03:12,260 --> 00:03:15,000
the locks all share the same basic design.

56
00:03:15,100 --> 00:03:17,200
You have three what they call tumblers or wheels

57
00:03:17,300 --> 00:03:18,600
inside the safe.

58
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:20,940
Each wheel has a notch on it to line up

59
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:22,940
with a part called the fence.

60
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:25,240
Once you have all three lined up,

61
00:03:25,340 --> 00:03:27,740
the fence will drop into the gates of the wheels.

62
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:29,380
And when you continue turning the dial,

63
00:03:29,480 --> 00:03:34,860
will retract the bolt, thus opening the safe.

64
00:03:34,861 --> 00:03:36,620
These locks seem to pale in comparison

65
00:03:36,720 --> 00:03:38,560
to the one on this monstrous vault door.

66
00:04:04,117 --> 00:04:05,960
I'm not attacking the door.

67
00:04:05,961 --> 00:04:08,860
I'm attacking the lock.

68
00:04:08,861 --> 00:04:11,600
OK, that lock could be on a smaller vault door.

69
00:04:11,601 --> 00:04:12,760
I'm still attacking the lock.

70
00:04:21,640 --> 00:04:23,200
It took just over an hour and a half

71
00:04:23,201 --> 00:04:25,920
for Sitar to crack open the lock on this bank vault door.

72
00:04:30,060 --> 00:04:31,520
Though new bank vaults and doors

73
00:04:31,521 --> 00:04:34,320
are becoming more and more sophisticated,

74
00:04:34,321 --> 00:04:37,620
The lock itself has remained relatively unchanged.

75
00:04:37,720 --> 00:04:40,560
The concept of the mechanical combination lock

76
00:04:40,660 --> 00:04:43,360
hasn't changed in over 150 years.

77
00:04:43,460 --> 00:04:45,960
So that tells you something.

78
00:04:46,060 --> 00:04:48,260
While some companies focus on vaults and safes

79
00:04:48,360 --> 00:04:53,280
to protect valuables, Total Security Solutions

80
00:04:53,380 --> 00:04:57,180
in Fowlerville, Michigan, focuses on designing and building

81
00:04:57,280 --> 00:04:59,580
systems to specifically protect the workers that

82
00:04:59,680 --> 00:05:01,120
handle the money.

83
00:05:01,220 --> 00:05:03,680
Their enclosures say, keep out, too.

84
00:05:03,681 --> 00:05:06,680
manufacture and install, bullet-resistant barriers

85
00:05:06,780 --> 00:05:11,680
for banks, gas stations, credit unions, post offices,

86
00:05:11,780 --> 00:05:15,620
pharmacies, pretty much you name it.

87
00:05:15,720 --> 00:05:17,520
The most obvious part of the system

88
00:05:17,620 --> 00:05:20,440
is the transparent plastic that stands between the customer

89
00:05:20,540 --> 00:05:22,140
and the employee.

90
00:05:22,240 --> 00:05:26,140
This can be a level one or level two cell cast acrylic

91
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:29,440
or a level three polycarbonate material.

92
00:05:29,540 --> 00:05:33,140
The level one acrylic is designed to basically stop

93
00:05:33,141 --> 00:05:36,140
stop a small caliber bullet.

94
00:05:36,240 --> 00:05:39,680
Level two is designed to stop a .357.

95
00:05:39,780 --> 00:05:43,620
Level three is designed to either stop a .44 Magnum

96
00:05:43,720 --> 00:05:45,660
or a 12-gauge shotgun.

97
00:05:45,760 --> 00:05:48,580
Protection doesn't end with the bullet-resistant acrylic.

98
00:05:48,680 --> 00:05:51,360
It continues with bullet-resistant fiberglass.

99
00:05:51,460 --> 00:05:53,300
The fiberglass will typically be used

100
00:05:53,400 --> 00:05:56,860
any place that is not seen, any place

101
00:05:56,960 --> 00:05:59,860
that transparencies are not used.

102
00:05:59,960 --> 00:06:02,040
This multi-layer material delaminates

103
00:06:02,041 --> 00:06:04,680
when the bullet hits it, partially separating

104
00:06:04,780 --> 00:06:07,340
to absorb the energy of the impact.

105
00:06:07,440 --> 00:06:09,440
This is a bullet here that entered in.

106
00:06:09,540 --> 00:06:12,220
And if you can look at the side of the material,

107
00:06:12,320 --> 00:06:16,080
you can see all the different layers in here.

108
00:06:16,180 --> 00:06:19,860
These layers ensure that the bullet never gets through.

109
00:06:19,960 --> 00:06:23,260
The most common customers for these shields are banks.

110
00:06:23,360 --> 00:06:26,000
This is what's called the freestanding system.

111
00:06:26,100 --> 00:06:29,100
It's more inviting, more friendly than something

112
00:06:29,101 --> 00:06:32,140
where you have a big old steel profile.

113
00:06:32,240 --> 00:06:36,080
What we're looking at here is called an arch window system.

114
00:06:36,180 --> 00:06:38,480
This design allows natural voice transmission

115
00:06:38,580 --> 00:06:41,320
while maintaining a tight overlap that keeps out anyone

116
00:06:41,420 --> 00:06:45,780
trying to physically reach or shoot through.

117
00:06:45,880 --> 00:06:47,580
If somebody puts a gun in here, we

118
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:50,760
have the buttresses there or the piece of glass here

119
00:06:50,860 --> 00:06:52,860
so that nothing can be shot through.

120
00:06:52,960 --> 00:06:56,060
And with the return wall section,

121
00:06:56,160 --> 00:06:59,100
nobody on either side can be shot.

122
00:06:59,101 --> 00:07:02,000
But the people working behind the barrier still need to be

123
00:07:02,100 --> 00:07:03,900
able to interact with their customers.

124
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:06,700
To do this safely, they installed deal trays,

125
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:09,140
which allow customers to pass money or paperwork

126
00:07:09,240 --> 00:07:10,640
through the barrier.

127
00:07:10,740 --> 00:07:12,740
For transactions that are a bit larger,

128
00:07:12,840 --> 00:07:15,040
they use a package passer.

129
00:07:15,140 --> 00:07:18,220
The basic premise of this is that only one door

130
00:07:18,320 --> 00:07:20,660
will open at a time.

131
00:07:20,760 --> 00:07:23,200
It has an interlocking mechanism in here.

132
00:07:23,300 --> 00:07:26,200
And what you do is you open this door,

133
00:07:26,300 --> 00:07:29,100
you put your coin in here, put your product in here,

134
00:07:29,101 --> 00:07:30,900
this door won't open.

135
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:34,400
Once this door is shut and closed, this door opens.

136
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:36,400
This door won't open.

137
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:38,840
This is all bullet-resistant acrylic.

138
00:07:38,940 --> 00:07:42,040
We can make these in any size, height, width, depth.

139
00:07:47,560 --> 00:07:49,160
Each part of the barrier system

140
00:07:49,260 --> 00:07:52,460
is cut and fabricated here at the Total Security shop.

141
00:07:54,960 --> 00:07:56,960
They start out with a sheet of acrylic.

142
00:07:59,100 --> 00:08:02,700
Once the saw cuts the piece into the specific size,

143
00:08:02,800 --> 00:08:04,940
another machine drills any notches or holes

144
00:08:05,040 --> 00:08:06,600
required for installation.

145
00:08:08,680 --> 00:08:11,340
Once that's done, it'll come over and it'll be sanded.

146
00:08:11,440 --> 00:08:14,140
And we'll get it to a nice, milky, white finish.

147
00:08:16,780 --> 00:08:18,860
The piece then has all the sharp edges

148
00:08:18,960 --> 00:08:21,460
filed down and flame polished.

149
00:08:21,560 --> 00:08:24,160
When we flame it, we're heating up this material

150
00:08:24,260 --> 00:08:27,400
and actually kind of melting it clear again.

151
00:08:27,401 --> 00:08:31,100
Acrylic is a bit more expensive than bullet-resistant glass,

152
00:08:31,200 --> 00:08:34,440
but weighs half as much and is easier to work with.

153
00:08:34,540 --> 00:08:37,440
These systems keep people safe in a relatively small

154
00:08:37,540 --> 00:08:38,340
environment.

155
00:08:41,240 --> 00:08:43,880
But down in the desert of New Mexico,

156
00:08:43,980 --> 00:08:47,520
the Department of Energy is using a 16-square-mile area

157
00:08:47,620 --> 00:08:49,680
to create an entirely different kind of barrier

158
00:08:49,780 --> 00:08:50,720
to keep people safe.

159
00:08:53,220 --> 00:08:56,700
This is the Waste Isolation Pilot Plant, or WIPP,

160
00:08:56,701 --> 00:08:59,900
and the staff is hard at work disposing of intermediate level

161
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:03,440
nuclear waste half a mile beneath the Earth's surface

162
00:09:03,540 --> 00:09:05,340
in a salt cavern.

163
00:09:05,440 --> 00:09:07,280
This nuclear waste must be isolated

164
00:09:07,380 --> 00:09:09,740
for tens of thousands of years.

165
00:09:09,840 --> 00:09:12,380
It's extremely dangerous.

166
00:09:12,480 --> 00:09:16,060
In 1999, the whip disposal site opened,

167
00:09:16,160 --> 00:09:19,860
and the waste has been arriving steadily ever since.

168
00:09:19,960 --> 00:09:22,620
Everything is designed to make sure the waste gets here

169
00:09:22,720 --> 00:09:26,700
without incident, even the shipping containers.

170
00:09:26,701 --> 00:09:29,340
They have to undergo significant testing.

171
00:09:29,440 --> 00:09:32,440
The two-pack, too, is dropped from about 30 feet

172
00:09:32,540 --> 00:09:34,240
in multiple configurations.

173
00:09:34,340 --> 00:09:36,340
After it's been dropped numerous times,

174
00:09:36,440 --> 00:09:41,920
it's put into an engulfing fire of jet fuel for about 30 minutes.

175
00:09:42,020 --> 00:09:44,620
Once the waste is in place, the salt cavern

176
00:09:44,720 --> 00:09:47,620
will slowly close upon itself, crushing the barrels

177
00:09:47,720 --> 00:09:52,700
in less than 1,000 years, sealing them off completely.

178
00:09:52,800 --> 00:09:54,520
What happens in the meantime?

179
00:09:54,521 --> 00:09:56,520
First, there's high security.

180
00:09:56,620 --> 00:09:59,420
Allowing nuclear waste to fall into the hands of terrorists

181
00:09:59,520 --> 00:10:01,320
could be catastrophic.

182
00:10:01,420 --> 00:10:03,320
We do have security officers that

183
00:10:03,420 --> 00:10:05,260
are manning the gates and the entryways

184
00:10:05,360 --> 00:10:06,460
for people coming on board.

185
00:10:06,560 --> 00:10:07,660
We do random patrols.

186
00:10:07,760 --> 00:10:08,960
And we also have some officers that

187
00:10:09,060 --> 00:10:12,380
do monitoring and surveillance using technology and equipment.

188
00:10:12,480 --> 00:10:14,100
But what if security is long gone

189
00:10:14,200 --> 00:10:16,940
before the waste becomes safe?

190
00:10:17,040 --> 00:10:19,420
In order to protect future generations,

191
00:10:19,520 --> 00:10:22,180
the site will need keep out signs that can last thousands

192
00:10:22,280 --> 00:10:24,520
of years.

193
00:10:24,521 --> 00:10:27,720
One of the requirements DOE had to demonstrate to the US

194
00:10:27,820 --> 00:10:30,660
Environmental Protection Agency, even before WIP was allowed

195
00:10:30,760 --> 00:10:35,340
to open, was that we could have a system of controls in place

196
00:10:35,440 --> 00:10:39,700
to keep people out for up to 10,000 years.

197
00:10:39,800 --> 00:10:42,300
Try thinking up a keep out sign that will last as long

198
00:10:42,400 --> 00:10:44,180
as civilization.

199
00:10:44,280 --> 00:10:47,540
The use of symbolism, it's a tricky art form.

200
00:10:47,640 --> 00:10:51,480
For one culture, the skull and crossbones may represent a hazard

201
00:10:51,580 --> 00:10:52,780
or stay away.

202
00:10:52,781 --> 00:10:57,260
Other cultures see that as a symbol of life.

203
00:10:57,360 --> 00:11:02,160
The best example we have today is the red, yellow, green symbol.

204
00:11:02,260 --> 00:11:04,400
That's almost known internationally for stop,

205
00:11:04,500 --> 00:11:07,200
go, take caution.

206
00:11:07,300 --> 00:11:10,060
The plan is to have 48 surface markers all along

207
00:11:10,160 --> 00:11:12,740
the perimeter and inside the facility.

208
00:11:12,840 --> 00:11:14,460
An information center will be located

209
00:11:14,560 --> 00:11:16,600
in the middle of the site.

210
00:11:16,700 --> 00:11:19,540
Both the markers and center will have warnings in six languages

211
00:11:19,541 --> 00:11:22,640
of the UN and Navajo, an indigenous language to the

212
00:11:22,740 --> 00:11:24,440
region.

213
00:11:24,540 --> 00:11:27,280
The lessons we sort of borrowed from are the Rosetta Stone,

214
00:11:27,380 --> 00:11:31,560
being able to decipher and decrypt some of those symbols.

215
00:11:31,660 --> 00:11:34,160
And we want to follow what we've learned from history

216
00:11:34,260 --> 00:11:37,800
and replicate it as much as possible.

217
00:11:37,900 --> 00:11:39,520
The disposal process is expected

218
00:11:39,620 --> 00:11:43,060
to be complete around 2030, and the site will subsequently

219
00:11:43,160 --> 00:11:46,500
close with security officers remaining.

220
00:11:46,600 --> 00:11:49,540
By that time, the cavern will contain all intermediate levels

221
00:11:49,541 --> 00:11:55,440
or transuranic waste in the US, about 4 million cubic feet.

222
00:11:55,540 --> 00:11:57,020
To put it another way...

223
00:11:57,120 --> 00:11:58,880
That's about the same amount of waste

224
00:11:58,980 --> 00:12:01,520
that you would have if you put a football field out there

225
00:12:01,620 --> 00:12:05,360
and fill that football field about 50 feet high.

226
00:12:05,460 --> 00:12:09,660
Still, there's 45,000 tons of high-level nuclear waste

227
00:12:09,760 --> 00:12:14,260
in the United States that requires permanent disposal.

228
00:12:14,360 --> 00:12:16,540
Any site that proposes to contain it

229
00:12:16,541 --> 00:12:18,200
will have to be even more secure.

230
00:12:20,940 --> 00:12:22,840
Signs, guards, and gates keep people

231
00:12:22,940 --> 00:12:26,380
out of this high-powered hazard.

232
00:12:26,480 --> 00:12:28,980
But others keep things safe with locks.

233
00:12:29,080 --> 00:12:31,260
And now the key may be no further away

234
00:12:31,360 --> 00:12:32,520
than the tip of your nose.

235
00:12:35,460 --> 00:12:39,200
In 2007, Jeff Sitar won his eighth consecutive world safe

236
00:12:39,300 --> 00:12:43,440
cracking title by cracking two safes in one hour, 34 minutes,

237
00:12:43,540 --> 00:12:44,840
and 10 seconds.

238
00:12:44,841 --> 00:12:46,340
He hasn't competed since.

239
00:12:50,840 --> 00:12:52,540
If you have something to protect,

240
00:12:52,640 --> 00:12:55,280
you'll do whatever you can to keep out intruders.

241
00:12:55,380 --> 00:13:00,880
Vaults, barriers, guards, fences, and signs

242
00:13:00,980 --> 00:13:03,500
all help to keep trespassers out.

243
00:13:03,600 --> 00:13:06,200
But when it comes to your home, sometimes all you need

244
00:13:06,300 --> 00:13:07,600
is a good lock.

245
00:13:07,700 --> 00:13:11,900
That's where companies like Master Lock come in.

246
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:13,700
This starts with an understanding

247
00:13:13,701 --> 00:13:16,240
that when a homeowner buys a lock for their front door

248
00:13:16,340 --> 00:13:17,740
or back door, what they're really buying

249
00:13:17,840 --> 00:13:19,340
is security for their home.

250
00:13:19,440 --> 00:13:21,340
And I guess even more specifically, what they're

251
00:13:21,440 --> 00:13:23,340
buying is peace of mind.

252
00:13:23,440 --> 00:13:25,080
For many, that peace of mind comes

253
00:13:25,180 --> 00:13:26,740
from a deadbolt on the door.

254
00:13:26,840 --> 00:13:28,480
But when you need to keep people out of something

255
00:13:28,580 --> 00:13:32,520
other than your home, you may look to a couple other locking

256
00:13:32,620 --> 00:13:34,260
devices.

257
00:13:34,360 --> 00:13:36,860
One of the products that Master Lock is most well known

258
00:13:36,960 --> 00:13:40,060
for is our laminated padlock.

259
00:13:40,160 --> 00:13:42,420
The laminated padlock is a key lock.

260
00:13:42,421 --> 00:13:45,220
The strength of the padlock comes from the layers

261
00:13:45,320 --> 00:13:48,360
of high-quality steel that are stacked together

262
00:13:48,460 --> 00:13:53,960
to provide extreme protection against physical attack.

263
00:13:54,060 --> 00:13:57,400
Attacks like the one shown in their famous 1974 Super Bowl

264
00:13:57,500 --> 00:13:58,400
commercial.

265
00:13:58,500 --> 00:14:00,840
The Tough Under Fire commercial featured

266
00:14:00,940 --> 00:14:05,740
an expert marksman using a .308 caliber rifle

267
00:14:05,840 --> 00:14:11,480
and firing a bullet through the center of this laminated lock.

268
00:14:11,481 --> 00:14:16,880
Master locks are tough under fire.

269
00:14:16,980 --> 00:14:18,880
This strength begins with the process

270
00:14:18,980 --> 00:14:22,720
of stacking specific stamped pieces together.

271
00:14:22,820 --> 00:14:24,620
Inside each one of these lock bodies

272
00:14:24,720 --> 00:14:28,160
is approximately 24 plates.

273
00:14:28,260 --> 00:14:30,540
What we have here is the progression of the tool,

274
00:14:30,640 --> 00:14:33,540
the first operation being the piercing of the rivet holes,

275
00:14:33,640 --> 00:14:35,780
then the octagonal shackle holes.

276
00:14:35,880 --> 00:14:37,300
And further down the line, we get

277
00:14:37,400 --> 00:14:39,700
into some of the internal components of the lock.

278
00:14:39,701 --> 00:14:42,700
So one lock laminate sticks to the next laminate.

279
00:14:42,800 --> 00:14:44,240
At the end of the day, what's happening

280
00:14:44,340 --> 00:14:47,040
is these things are being stacked together like LEGOs,

281
00:14:47,140 --> 00:14:49,740
essentially.

282
00:14:49,840 --> 00:14:52,140
After Master Lock constructs the body,

283
00:14:52,240 --> 00:14:54,040
it assembles the internal components.

284
00:14:57,160 --> 00:14:58,660
The very first thing that we're doing

285
00:14:58,760 --> 00:15:00,360
is we're loading the plugs.

286
00:15:00,460 --> 00:15:02,460
Six pins are loaded into the plug

287
00:15:02,560 --> 00:15:04,760
to set the combination of the lock.

288
00:15:04,860 --> 00:15:08,660
All told, there's about 250,000 different combinations

289
00:15:08,661 --> 00:15:12,700
that we can make on this machine, given all the pin combinations that we have.

290
00:15:16,060 --> 00:15:20,900
Over on the right side of the machine, we're loading the brass shell onto the machine.

291
00:15:20,901 --> 00:15:25,220
It indexes to the next station. It loads to six springs.

292
00:15:25,221 --> 00:15:29,420
And after that, it indexes again. It loads the six driver pins.

293
00:15:31,420 --> 00:15:36,780
At the center of the machine, we push the shell onto the plug, marry the two together,

294
00:15:36,781 --> 00:15:38,580
And this is the completed cylinder.

295
00:15:43,380 --> 00:15:46,520
All told, there are 20 different components

296
00:15:46,620 --> 00:15:48,360
inside this cylinder.

297
00:15:48,460 --> 00:15:51,220
Six driver pins, six tumbler pins, six springs,

298
00:15:51,320 --> 00:15:53,540
a shell, and a plug.

299
00:15:53,640 --> 00:15:55,640
The user inserts the key.

300
00:15:55,740 --> 00:15:58,040
The notches on the key align the pins,

301
00:15:58,140 --> 00:16:00,540
allowing the cylinder to turn and open the lock.

302
00:16:00,541 --> 00:16:06,540
Master Lock puts their locks to the test to make sure

303
00:16:06,640 --> 00:16:08,180
that they don't fail.

304
00:16:08,280 --> 00:16:12,980
They bend, cut, hammer, and pull on their locks

305
00:16:13,080 --> 00:16:15,380
to see just how strong they are.

306
00:16:15,480 --> 00:16:19,200
They even test against the forces of Mother Nature.

307
00:16:19,300 --> 00:16:21,800
One of the tests we subject our products

308
00:16:21,900 --> 00:16:24,100
to is weather resistance.

309
00:16:24,200 --> 00:16:28,800
So test methods that replicate salt spray, oceanic type

310
00:16:28,801 --> 00:16:32,800
coastal climates, we subject our products to.

311
00:16:32,900 --> 00:16:34,800
But padlocks aren't the only devices

312
00:16:34,900 --> 00:16:36,800
that Master Lock is famous for.

313
00:16:36,900 --> 00:16:40,440
They also produce a rotary dial combination lock.

314
00:16:40,540 --> 00:16:43,340
Most consumers have their first experience

315
00:16:43,440 --> 00:16:46,860
with the Master Lock brand in that it is the most widely

316
00:16:46,960 --> 00:16:50,360
used locker lock in schools and institutions today.

317
00:16:53,100 --> 00:16:54,900
At their North America manufacturing

318
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:58,000
facilities, Master Lock produces 9 million

319
00:16:58,001 --> 00:17:01,340
combination locks per year, half of which are made at their

320
00:17:01,440 --> 00:17:03,500
manufacturing plant in Milwaukee, Wisconsin.

321
00:17:06,740 --> 00:17:09,740
Each lock consists of about 20 different components.

322
00:17:09,840 --> 00:17:13,040
All 20 components that go into this lock are manufactured here

323
00:17:13,140 --> 00:17:14,240
in Milwaukee.

324
00:17:14,340 --> 00:17:16,560
From the auto press department behind me,

325
00:17:16,660 --> 00:17:19,960
they all get fed into this machine through a combination

326
00:17:20,060 --> 00:17:22,160
of probably 40 stations.

327
00:17:22,260 --> 00:17:25,160
And one part at a time gets assembled into the lock

328
00:17:25,260 --> 00:17:28,000
until it finally comes off the end as a package.

329
00:17:28,001 --> 00:17:30,500
tested lock.

330
00:17:30,600 --> 00:17:33,500
This machine turns out a lock every two seconds,

331
00:17:33,600 --> 00:17:37,000
producing about 18,000 combination locks a day.

332
00:17:37,100 --> 00:17:39,740
But it does more than just put them together.

333
00:17:39,840 --> 00:17:41,540
It also makes sure they work.

334
00:17:41,640 --> 00:17:43,540
One of the unique features of this machine

335
00:17:43,640 --> 00:17:47,760
is its ability to be able to understand the combination,

336
00:17:47,860 --> 00:17:51,060
auto dial, test, open and close the lock,

337
00:17:51,160 --> 00:17:53,760
all within the two-second cycle time of the machine.

338
00:17:53,761 --> 00:17:56,560
the machine.

339
00:17:56,660 --> 00:17:58,560
Whether it's a deadbolt on your door

340
00:17:58,660 --> 00:18:01,300
or a padlock on your garage, a strong lock

341
00:18:01,400 --> 00:18:04,400
can mean the difference in someone getting in or being

342
00:18:04,500 --> 00:18:06,500
kept out.

343
00:18:06,600 --> 00:18:09,300
Each lock has a unique key to open it.

344
00:18:09,400 --> 00:18:13,720
But what happens when that key gets in the wrong hands?

345
00:18:13,820 --> 00:18:15,720
To allow the right people easy access

346
00:18:15,820 --> 00:18:18,320
and keep the wrong people out, companies

347
00:18:18,420 --> 00:18:20,860
are looking to biometrics.

348
00:18:20,861 --> 00:18:25,700
It's a field that involves keys that can't be lost or stolen.

349
00:18:25,800 --> 00:18:29,340
Biometrics is a means of identifying an individual

350
00:18:29,440 --> 00:18:34,880
based on a physical attribute or biological attribute.

351
00:18:34,980 --> 00:18:36,580
The idea of systematic screening

352
00:18:36,680 --> 00:18:39,920
by identifying unique body features like scars or tattoos

353
00:18:40,020 --> 00:18:41,320
has been around for millennia.

354
00:18:44,820 --> 00:18:48,620
4,000 years ago, Babylonians used fingerprints on tablets

355
00:18:48,621 --> 00:18:54,020
to close business deals. The attacks of 9-11 created a

356
00:18:54,021 --> 00:18:59,060
greater need for biometric securities. This led to the

357
00:18:59,061 --> 00:19:01,940
establishment of Biometrics Services International in

358
00:19:01,941 --> 00:19:05,340
Morgantown, West Virginia, a subsidiary of the National

359
00:19:05,341 --> 00:19:08,940
Biometric Security Project. Biometrics is the

360
00:19:08,941 --> 00:19:12,980
highest form of security technology that you can

361
00:19:12,981 --> 00:19:15,740
currently provide. One form of biometric

362
00:19:15,741 --> 00:19:19,580
security gaining popularity is facial recognition.

363
00:19:19,680 --> 00:19:22,840
While already a popular way to find and identify criminals,

364
00:19:22,940 --> 00:19:25,180
it's being adapted for door locks.

365
00:19:25,280 --> 00:19:28,680
This is a 3D facial recognition device.

366
00:19:28,780 --> 00:19:31,080
This right here is the enrollment station.

367
00:19:31,180 --> 00:19:33,900
You would see something much like this on the door

368
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:35,900
if you were coming in to verify.

369
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:40,600
But this would be your initial stop in the process.

370
00:19:40,700 --> 00:19:43,140
Enrollment allows each device to recognize the face

371
00:19:43,141 --> 00:19:45,940
the face of the user to authorize access.

372
00:19:45,941 --> 00:19:50,380
On the facial recognition device, enrollment means merely getting your picture taken.

373
00:19:50,381 --> 00:19:56,020
Obviously it focuses on the eyes, on the brow, nose, mouth, and chin.

374
00:19:56,021 --> 00:20:01,360
And what it will do, it will take measurements in between these points.

375
00:20:01,361 --> 00:20:05,760
The computer selects different points on your face and develops a map between them,

376
00:20:05,761 --> 00:20:09,320
converting them into a numerical string or code.

377
00:20:09,321 --> 00:20:13,980
This creates a template that the computer will use to identify the person trying to pass.

378
00:20:13,981 --> 00:20:19,000
So from then on, when you present your face to this device, you stare at yourself for

379
00:20:19,001 --> 00:20:22,140
a split second and you're in.

380
00:20:22,141 --> 00:20:26,580
While this device uses several points on the face to identify the user, this one uses

381
00:20:26,581 --> 00:20:28,040
only the eye.

382
00:20:28,041 --> 00:20:31,320
Over here, we have an iris recognition system.

383
00:20:31,321 --> 00:20:33,320
It basically takes a picture of your iris.

384
00:20:33,321 --> 00:20:38,320
It will make, again, a numerical representation of your iris and then store that.

385
00:20:38,321 --> 00:20:41,920
Much like a fingerprint, each iris is unique.

386
00:20:42,020 --> 00:20:44,300
It has splits, swirls, and different breakpoints

387
00:20:44,400 --> 00:20:46,260
that the computer measures and maps,

388
00:20:46,360 --> 00:20:48,960
similar to the facial recognition program.

389
00:20:49,060 --> 00:20:50,760
All the user must do from then on

390
00:20:50,860 --> 00:20:53,660
is stand in the right spot, and access is granted.

391
00:20:53,760 --> 00:20:57,600
Identification is completed.

392
00:20:57,700 --> 00:21:00,680
But don't try to fool the system with a stolen eyeball.

393
00:21:00,780 --> 00:21:03,580
This does have a liveness detection system in it.

394
00:21:03,680 --> 00:21:06,180
It will check to make sure that the iris in front of it

395
00:21:06,280 --> 00:21:07,680
is actually alive.

396
00:21:07,681 --> 00:21:10,780
You can't pull someone's eyeball out like you see in the movies

397
00:21:10,880 --> 00:21:12,720
and stick it up to this sort of device.

398
00:21:12,820 --> 00:21:15,420
The iris disintegrates very, very quickly after death.

399
00:21:19,420 --> 00:21:21,920
One of the most widely recognized biometrics

400
00:21:22,020 --> 00:21:24,740
is fingerprint recognition.

401
00:21:24,840 --> 00:21:29,440
Identifying fingerprints dates back to the late 19th century.

402
00:21:29,540 --> 00:21:32,780
By the 1920s, fingerprints were used by law enforcement,

403
00:21:32,880 --> 00:21:37,680
the US military, and the FBI as a means of identification.

404
00:21:37,681 --> 00:21:40,680
Using a fingerprint as a way to determine who gets in and who's

405
00:21:40,681 --> 00:21:44,680
kept out works the same way as the iris recognition device.

406
00:21:44,681 --> 00:21:48,680
This will look at the ridge patterns on your fingerprints,

407
00:21:48,681 --> 00:21:52,680
where this one ends, where this one ends, where these two split

408
00:21:52,681 --> 00:21:55,680
right here. That could be a place for a marking point.

409
00:21:55,681 --> 00:22:00,680
Want your system super secure? Try a three-factor fingerprint

410
00:22:00,681 --> 00:22:04,680
identification device. How the system works, you'll come up,

411
00:22:04,681 --> 00:22:06,680
you'll insert your card in here,

412
00:22:06,681 --> 00:22:08,680
you'll enter your PIN number here.

413
00:22:08,681 --> 00:22:10,680
The PIN number will be the PIN number that matches on the card,

414
00:22:10,681 --> 00:22:13,680
and it will prompt you to put your fingerprint on the sensor

415
00:22:13,681 --> 00:22:14,680
down here.

416
00:22:14,681 --> 00:22:16,680
And then if all those things match up, it will go ahead and grant

417
00:22:16,681 --> 00:22:18,680
you access.

418
00:22:18,681 --> 00:22:21,680
This system uses a smart card that features a memory chip

419
00:22:21,681 --> 00:22:24,680
containing a biometric template of the user.

420
00:22:24,681 --> 00:22:27,680
The fingerprint must match the template.

421
00:22:27,681 --> 00:22:30,680
Only then will the user gain access.

422
00:22:30,681 --> 00:22:33,680
By the way, don't get any ideas about using someone else's

423
00:22:33,681 --> 00:22:37,680
someone else's finger while it's not on their hand.

424
00:22:37,780 --> 00:22:39,880
Much like the iris scanner, these systems also

425
00:22:39,980 --> 00:22:42,480
feature liveness detection.

426
00:22:42,580 --> 00:22:45,020
All these devices rely on physical patterns that

427
00:22:45,120 --> 00:22:46,720
can be seen on the body.

428
00:22:46,820 --> 00:22:48,420
But what about a device that sees patterns

429
00:22:48,520 --> 00:22:49,920
on the inside of the body?

430
00:22:50,020 --> 00:22:51,840
This is a vein recognition system

431
00:22:51,940 --> 00:22:53,240
that we have in front of us here.

432
00:22:53,340 --> 00:22:56,140
What this technology does is it does go through the skin.

433
00:22:56,240 --> 00:22:59,940
It does use an infrared light to read the actual veins going

434
00:23:00,040 --> 00:23:03,680
through your hand, and it will use those as its pattern.

435
00:23:03,681 --> 00:23:06,320
Like the facial and iris recognition devices,

436
00:23:06,420 --> 00:23:08,880
this uses the pattern to create a numerical code

437
00:23:08,980 --> 00:23:11,280
to grant access to the user.

438
00:23:11,380 --> 00:23:15,020
It is sort of like a hidden key.

439
00:23:15,120 --> 00:23:17,520
Biometric locks guarantee that the right people

440
00:23:17,620 --> 00:23:21,840
are getting in and the wrong people are being kept out.

441
00:23:21,940 --> 00:23:25,340
But when you're trying to secure a 2,000-mile border,

442
00:23:25,440 --> 00:23:28,880
you'll need another approach.

443
00:23:28,980 --> 00:23:33,680
The FBI's fingerprint database is the largest in the world.

444
00:23:33,681 --> 00:23:37,580
It receives 34,000 new fingerprint cards every day.

445
00:23:41,280 --> 00:23:46,020
In most cases, keeping someone out is an achievable goal,

446
00:23:46,120 --> 00:23:49,420
whether the restricted area is a safe, a nuclear waste site,

447
00:23:49,520 --> 00:23:51,540
or a cutting-edge lab.

448
00:23:51,640 --> 00:23:53,940
But what if the area is a whole country?

449
00:23:54,040 --> 00:23:56,640
Things get a lot tougher.

450
00:23:56,740 --> 00:23:59,140
Since 9-11, most governments around the world

451
00:23:59,240 --> 00:24:02,380
have beefed up keep-out policies and enforced them

452
00:24:02,381 --> 00:24:07,380
with border control. At a yearly cost of billions of

453
00:24:07,381 --> 00:24:11,380
dollars, the US Customs and Border Protection oversees

454
00:24:11,381 --> 00:24:17,020
nearly 2,000 miles of border between the US and Mexico.

455
00:24:17,021 --> 00:24:19,380
That distance makes it the world's longest heavily

456
00:24:19,381 --> 00:24:23,380
patrolled border between neighboring countries.

457
00:24:23,381 --> 00:24:27,380
Certain spots see much more activity than others.

458
00:24:27,381 --> 00:24:30,380
The San Ysidro port of entry just south of San Diego,

459
00:24:30,381 --> 00:24:33,720
California, manages a massive amount of border traffic.

460
00:24:38,720 --> 00:24:40,220
We're here at the San Diego Port of Entry,

461
00:24:40,320 --> 00:24:41,820
busiest land board in the world.

462
00:24:41,920 --> 00:24:44,120
We see over 40,000 to 50,000 cars a day,

463
00:24:44,220 --> 00:24:45,620
25,000 pedestrians.

464
00:24:45,720 --> 00:24:46,620
We add everybody up.

465
00:24:46,720 --> 00:24:51,340
It's just about 100,000 people a day through this one port.

466
00:24:51,440 --> 00:24:54,340
That's about 36 million people passing through this single

467
00:24:54,440 --> 00:24:56,940
border point each year.

468
00:24:57,040 --> 00:24:59,680
What the officers here are doing more than looking for unlawful

469
00:24:59,681 --> 00:25:00,580
entrance.

470
00:25:04,480 --> 00:25:06,860
They're on the hunt for terrorists, drugs,

471
00:25:06,960 --> 00:25:09,760
and banned agriculture.

472
00:25:09,860 --> 00:25:12,160
On an average day, we go between 100 and 200 people

473
00:25:12,260 --> 00:25:14,900
that we catch with violations of the law.

474
00:25:15,000 --> 00:25:17,460
These long lines might be a nuisance,

475
00:25:17,560 --> 00:25:21,340
but having them allows extra time to find violators.

476
00:25:21,440 --> 00:25:23,940
We have teams of officers who work with and without dogs,

477
00:25:24,040 --> 00:25:25,500
and they're capable of detecting a lot of things

478
00:25:25,600 --> 00:25:27,900
before you get to the booth.

479
00:25:27,901 --> 00:25:30,640
The dogs' keen sense of smell is key in sniffing out weapons

480
00:25:30,740 --> 00:25:32,840
and drugs, money and people.

481
00:25:35,840 --> 00:25:37,940
The dogs are just one of the filters Customs

482
00:25:38,040 --> 00:25:40,140
and Border Protection uses to screen intruders

483
00:25:40,240 --> 00:25:41,740
before they even reach an officer.

484
00:25:44,240 --> 00:25:47,760
But their system hasn't always been this efficient and secure.

485
00:25:47,860 --> 00:25:50,920
A lot has changed, especially since 9-11, like these poles,

486
00:25:51,020 --> 00:25:52,260
for example, we have out here.

487
00:25:52,360 --> 00:25:54,460
And that's to prevent people from going in between lanes,

488
00:25:54,560 --> 00:25:55,560
what we call port runners, trying

489
00:25:55,660 --> 00:25:57,900
to escape from the inspection process.

490
00:25:57,901 --> 00:26:00,140
This is our radio frequency ID reader.

491
00:26:00,240 --> 00:26:02,840
And what this does for us, people can drive past,

492
00:26:02,940 --> 00:26:04,340
and we'll have their information before they even

493
00:26:04,440 --> 00:26:08,040
get to the officer at the primary booth.

494
00:26:08,140 --> 00:26:10,040
This is our license plate reader technology.

495
00:26:10,140 --> 00:26:12,240
So if there's a stolen car or anything else about the plate,

496
00:26:12,340 --> 00:26:13,840
we will know that before the person gets

497
00:26:13,940 --> 00:26:15,060
to the inspection booth.

498
00:26:17,660 --> 00:26:19,760
Those who pass the battery of inspections

499
00:26:19,860 --> 00:26:21,860
and document verification are allowed

500
00:26:21,960 --> 00:26:24,000
to enter the United States.

501
00:26:24,100 --> 00:26:26,260
Others go to a secondary inspection

502
00:26:26,261 --> 00:26:29,560
inspection, where vehicles are subjected to a more detailed

503
00:26:29,561 --> 00:26:30,400
search.

504
00:26:30,401 --> 00:26:33,000
Sometimes they're even driven through an x-ray.

505
00:26:33,001 --> 00:26:35,200
As people drive through, we can actually x-ray the entire

506
00:26:35,201 --> 00:26:37,800
vehicle, both sides and the top, and see everything that's in

507
00:26:37,801 --> 00:26:39,800
the vehicle.

508
00:26:39,801 --> 00:26:42,600
Not all illegal crossings happen at the 39 land

509
00:26:42,601 --> 00:26:44,220
ports of entry.

510
00:26:44,221 --> 00:26:48,640
In fact, most violators try to avoid the ports altogether.

511
00:26:48,641 --> 00:26:50,860
That's where Border Patrol steps in.

512
00:26:50,861 --> 00:26:54,420
And in San Ysidro, it all starts with two distinct fences over

513
00:26:54,421 --> 00:26:57,460
miles in length. Anyone that's in this area,

514
00:26:57,461 --> 00:27:01,360
we know they're either with us or they're here illegally.

515
00:27:01,361 --> 00:27:05,260
Prior to 1994, this area was overrun by alien traffic.

516
00:27:05,261 --> 00:27:08,360
We had groups in the hundreds that would make bonsai runs,

517
00:27:08,361 --> 00:27:10,900
and we didn't have enough manpower, technology,

518
00:27:10,901 --> 00:27:14,400
infrastructure, anything to withstand that kind of alien

519
00:27:14,401 --> 00:27:18,440
traffic. As of 2008,

520
00:27:18,441 --> 00:27:22,440
500 miles of fencing from California to Texas stands

521
00:27:22,441 --> 00:27:25,380
stands between the United States and Mexico.

522
00:27:25,381 --> 00:27:28,040
More fencing is planned.

523
00:27:28,041 --> 00:27:30,080
Even though extra fencing and frequent patrols

524
00:27:30,081 --> 00:27:32,780
have slowed illegal traffic near the San Ysidro border,

525
00:27:32,781 --> 00:27:35,580
the area is not without its dangers.

526
00:27:35,581 --> 00:27:39,160
We've just taken out over 2,000 feet of slats here.

527
00:27:39,161 --> 00:27:41,400
The reason why we did that is because we have so many assaults

528
00:27:41,401 --> 00:27:42,820
in this area.

529
00:27:42,821 --> 00:27:45,060
We wanted to know where these individuals were standing

530
00:27:45,061 --> 00:27:47,300
when they were throwing these large rocks and bottles

531
00:27:47,301 --> 00:27:48,760
at our agents.

532
00:27:48,761 --> 00:27:51,300
A quarter of all assaults in the United States

533
00:27:51,301 --> 00:27:54,540
happened in San Diego, and close to 80% of those assaults

534
00:27:54,541 --> 00:27:56,680
happened in the area that we're in.

535
00:27:56,681 --> 00:27:59,740
To contend with individuals jumping the primary fence,

536
00:27:59,741 --> 00:28:02,520
the Army Corps of Engineers constructed a secondary fence

537
00:28:02,521 --> 00:28:05,020
for the Border Patrol Agency.

538
00:28:05,021 --> 00:28:06,940
Behind me is a secondary fence.

539
00:28:06,941 --> 00:28:11,080
It was put up in 1996 to help us control this area.

540
00:28:11,081 --> 00:28:13,920
On top of it, we put on concertina wire

541
00:28:13,921 --> 00:28:17,220
towards the end of 2007.

542
00:28:17,221 --> 00:28:21,120
But this five-mile fence still doesn't deter some.

543
00:28:21,121 --> 00:28:24,360
This place has actually had a number of different cuts

544
00:28:24,361 --> 00:28:26,740
in the fence where individuals will come up here,

545
00:28:26,741 --> 00:28:31,180
strike it with some type of an ax just to start the hole.

546
00:28:31,181 --> 00:28:33,760
And then they'll come through with an electric saw

547
00:28:33,761 --> 00:28:37,320
or circular saw and then cut a hole in the fence.

548
00:28:37,321 --> 00:28:39,520
In this specific area, we've actually doubled up

549
00:28:39,521 --> 00:28:41,480
and put the same material on both sides

550
00:28:41,481 --> 00:28:43,560
because it's so heavily compromised.

551
00:28:43,561 --> 00:28:46,840
The nice thing about having the fence the way it is

552
00:28:46,841 --> 00:28:48,260
is for anyone to compromise, it's

553
00:28:48,261 --> 00:28:49,520
going to take several minutes.

554
00:28:49,521 --> 00:28:52,920
And so that allows us to respond to the area.

555
00:28:52,921 --> 00:28:56,620
The road here may be smooth, but most of the border areas

556
00:28:56,621 --> 00:28:58,720
involve rougher terrain.

557
00:28:58,721 --> 00:29:00,920
Officers must use ATVs and horses

558
00:29:00,921 --> 00:29:04,620
to patrol and secure those areas.

559
00:29:04,621 --> 00:29:07,720
Still, the border patrol uses more than just eyes on the ground.

560
00:29:10,420 --> 00:29:12,880
This is a remote video surveillance system control

561
00:29:12,881 --> 00:29:14,020
center.

562
00:29:14,021 --> 00:29:17,380
It's one of many centers stationed along the border.

563
00:29:17,381 --> 00:29:19,180
The activity that gets observed here

564
00:29:19,181 --> 00:29:22,020
to the agents in the field so that they can go and respond

565
00:29:22,120 --> 00:29:26,420
to it and take care of whatever's happening out in the area.

566
00:29:26,520 --> 00:29:28,920
The camera system monitors a five-mile stretch

567
00:29:29,020 --> 00:29:31,060
between two ports of entry.

568
00:29:31,160 --> 00:29:33,300
Each operator views four camera feeds,

569
00:29:33,400 --> 00:29:37,000
depending on the level of activity.

570
00:29:37,100 --> 00:29:39,640
But the darkness of night provides additional challenges.

571
00:29:42,540 --> 00:29:45,280
Positioned on high hilltops are night vision scopes,

572
00:29:45,380 --> 00:29:47,700
equipped with infrared technology.

573
00:29:47,701 --> 00:29:50,700
Each scope is monitored by an agent from his vehicle.

574
00:29:50,800 --> 00:29:53,700
The scope can see about a mile out for precise people

575
00:29:53,800 --> 00:29:56,440
and still be able to count how many people are running

576
00:29:56,540 --> 00:29:58,240
or how many people are crossing the border.

577
00:29:58,340 --> 00:30:02,540
It's in motion control just like this, up, down, left, right.

578
00:30:02,640 --> 00:30:06,180
And also you can zoom in on the individuals and the area

579
00:30:06,280 --> 00:30:08,180
if you think it's suspect.

580
00:30:08,280 --> 00:30:10,660
This isn't just a night vision video game.

581
00:30:10,760 --> 00:30:12,520
The stakes are high.

582
00:30:12,620 --> 00:30:14,320
You've got to know your area really well

583
00:30:14,420 --> 00:30:16,160
to be able to relay to the people who are down there

584
00:30:16,161 --> 00:30:18,800
down there on the field and tell them exactly how to come in.

585
00:30:18,900 --> 00:30:19,760
They can't see anything.

586
00:30:19,860 --> 00:30:20,900
It's dark for them.

587
00:30:21,000 --> 00:30:22,660
All they know is that there's someone out there

588
00:30:22,760 --> 00:30:24,400
and it may be close to them.

589
00:30:24,500 --> 00:30:27,940
In spite of all these sophisticated measures,

590
00:30:28,040 --> 00:30:31,780
some organizations estimate that over 500,000 illegally

591
00:30:31,880 --> 00:30:33,440
cross the US border each year.

592
00:30:37,980 --> 00:30:39,520
While Customs and Border Protection

593
00:30:39,620 --> 00:30:42,780
utilizes perimeters and barriers,

594
00:30:42,880 --> 00:30:44,920
many others use them as well.

595
00:30:44,921 --> 00:30:48,560
Places of high security like banks, military facilities,

596
00:30:48,660 --> 00:30:51,720
and chemical plants all depend on barriers as a means

597
00:30:51,820 --> 00:30:53,860
of keeping out intruders.

598
00:30:53,960 --> 00:30:57,300
Most are permanent barriers in the form of large blocks,

599
00:30:57,400 --> 00:31:00,060
while others are movable, allowing access

600
00:31:00,160 --> 00:31:02,340
to approved visitors.

601
00:31:02,440 --> 00:31:04,200
One thing they all have in common

602
00:31:04,300 --> 00:31:07,040
is they have to be tested.

603
00:31:07,140 --> 00:31:08,540
Companies in the barrier business

604
00:31:08,640 --> 00:31:11,840
turn to the Federal Highway Administration and US Department

605
00:31:11,940 --> 00:31:14,920
of State to test and approve their products.

606
00:31:14,921 --> 00:31:20,760
crash after crash after crash.

607
00:31:20,860 --> 00:31:25,460
The current standard rating companies test for is the M50,

608
00:31:25,560 --> 00:31:29,060
meaning a barrier will stop a 15,000-pound vehicle traveling

609
00:31:29,160 --> 00:31:31,160
at 50 miles per hour.

610
00:31:31,260 --> 00:31:35,180
But there's more than one way to stop a moving truck.

611
00:31:35,280 --> 00:31:38,680
The ground retractable automobile barrier, or GRAB,

612
00:31:38,780 --> 00:31:42,180
is a technology developed by Universal Safety Response,

613
00:31:42,280 --> 00:31:44,080
or USR.

614
00:31:44,081 --> 00:31:46,840
It was developed after the creator and president of USR

615
00:31:46,841 --> 00:31:49,220
witnessed a fatal car crash.

616
00:31:49,221 --> 00:31:51,720
Shortly after that, I was watching the History Channel

617
00:31:51,721 --> 00:31:54,020
and I saw the old World War II aircrafts

618
00:31:54,021 --> 00:31:56,420
where if the planes missed the arresting wire,

619
00:31:56,421 --> 00:31:57,960
they got stopped by a net system.

620
00:31:57,961 --> 00:32:00,260
And I was thinking, if you can stop a plane,

621
00:32:00,261 --> 00:32:03,260
you ought to be able to stop a vehicle.

622
00:32:03,261 --> 00:32:06,860
USR designed the grab to be a non-lethal barrier.

623
00:32:06,861 --> 00:32:09,840
It also wanted to make the grab easy to operate.

624
00:32:09,841 --> 00:32:12,400
The grab system is an active vehicle barrier system.

625
00:32:12,401 --> 00:32:15,600
And what that means is it either allows or denies access

626
00:32:15,700 --> 00:32:19,900
to vehicles who are looking to enter or exit a facility.

627
00:32:20,000 --> 00:32:22,140
With a simple push of a button,

628
00:32:22,240 --> 00:32:25,940
the barrier raises or lowers in two seconds or less.

629
00:32:26,040 --> 00:32:31,120
But could a non-lethal net system work as well as other barriers?

630
00:32:31,220 --> 00:32:33,360
In addition to 14 successful crash tests,

631
00:32:33,460 --> 00:32:36,460
the grab system has also been struck over 115 times

632
00:32:36,560 --> 00:32:39,700
in the real world at systems installed across the country.

633
00:32:39,701 --> 00:32:42,940
and the vehicle occupants escaped without significant injuries,

634
00:32:43,040 --> 00:32:47,040
suffering no more than a seat belt bruise.

635
00:32:47,140 --> 00:32:50,580
Since opening in 2001, USR has delivered

636
00:32:50,680 --> 00:32:53,940
over 300 grab systems.

637
00:32:54,040 --> 00:32:55,540
While Customs and Border Protection

638
00:32:55,640 --> 00:32:59,720
stops terrorists on land, what kinds of technologies

639
00:32:59,820 --> 00:33:01,520
keep the unwanted out of our skies?

640
00:33:04,860 --> 00:33:07,120
The Great Wall of China is a series of walls

641
00:33:07,220 --> 00:33:09,360
more than twice the length of the United States

642
00:33:09,361 --> 00:33:10,160
border.

643
00:33:15,860 --> 00:33:18,400
Locks keep out intruders.

644
00:33:18,500 --> 00:33:21,000
Barriers also keep out the unwanted.

645
00:33:21,100 --> 00:33:23,000
But what if the approach is from the skies?

646
00:33:29,020 --> 00:33:31,720
This is Customs and Border Protection's Air and Marine

647
00:33:31,820 --> 00:33:37,020
Operations Center, or AMOC, in Riverside, California.

648
00:33:37,021 --> 00:33:40,320
The job of the staff here is to monitor all U.S. airspace

649
00:33:40,420 --> 00:33:45,360
for general aviation, 24 hours a day, seven days a week.

650
00:33:45,460 --> 00:33:48,560
Our responsibility is to know who they are

651
00:33:48,660 --> 00:33:50,460
and that they go where they're supposed to.

652
00:33:50,560 --> 00:33:52,640
Each detection enforcement officer here

653
00:33:52,740 --> 00:33:57,140
is responsible for a specific area, California, Arizona,

654
00:33:57,240 --> 00:34:00,040
New Mexico, Texas, and you can go across the southern border

655
00:34:00,140 --> 00:34:01,540
and then northern border.

656
00:34:07,020 --> 00:34:10,760
With more than 87,000 daily flights in the US,

657
00:34:10,860 --> 00:34:14,220
it's necessary to break up the surveillance into sections,

658
00:34:14,320 --> 00:34:16,220
especially during the day.

659
00:34:16,320 --> 00:34:18,460
Generally, that's when most people fly.

660
00:34:18,560 --> 00:34:22,800
So 16,000 aircraft, of which 77% of them

661
00:34:22,900 --> 00:34:25,800
are general aviation aircraft coming into the United States.

662
00:34:25,900 --> 00:34:28,800
So when you go out and look at an entire area,

663
00:34:28,900 --> 00:34:33,820
it's very difficult to pinpoint one specific aircraft.

664
00:34:33,920 --> 00:34:36,220
Radar is key to this operation.

665
00:34:36,221 --> 00:34:38,860
Combining ground-based and airborne radars,

666
00:34:38,960 --> 00:34:43,520
the facility is capable of displaying 450 radars at once.

667
00:34:43,620 --> 00:34:46,560
But this isn't just a big green scope that beeps.

668
00:34:46,660 --> 00:34:49,460
We're sitting in front of what we call the Air Marine

669
00:34:49,560 --> 00:34:52,060
Operations Surveillance System, the AMOS.

670
00:34:52,160 --> 00:34:55,880
It's our system that displays not only all of the radars

671
00:34:55,980 --> 00:35:00,580
that we have into the system, but also the air tracks.

672
00:35:00,680 --> 00:35:03,380
From this station, operators can select any plane

673
00:35:03,381 --> 00:35:06,140
plane and be able to see its flight pattern, where it

674
00:35:06,240 --> 00:35:09,320
departed, where it's supposed to land, as well as a two-year

675
00:35:09,420 --> 00:35:10,820
history of the aircraft.

676
00:35:10,920 --> 00:35:12,420
And if immediate action is needed,

677
00:35:12,520 --> 00:35:14,960
the system can even tell the operator where the plane will

678
00:35:15,060 --> 00:35:16,620
be in the near future.

679
00:35:16,720 --> 00:35:18,220
If it is of interest to me, then I

680
00:35:18,320 --> 00:35:22,140
want to find out where is the track going to be in, let's

681
00:35:22,240 --> 00:35:23,640
say, 10 minutes.

682
00:35:23,740 --> 00:35:24,940
I can also ask it that.

683
00:35:25,040 --> 00:35:28,140
And we'll display a 10-minute vector that will also allow me

684
00:35:28,240 --> 00:35:31,480
to tell whether that aircraft is maneuvering east, west,

685
00:35:31,580 --> 00:35:32,740
or whatever direction.

686
00:35:32,741 --> 00:35:35,540
Some sites in the United States

687
00:35:35,640 --> 00:35:37,840
get extra attention from the operators,

688
00:35:37,940 --> 00:35:39,380
like the nation's capital.

689
00:35:39,480 --> 00:35:41,220
When it comes to protecting airspace,

690
00:35:41,320 --> 00:35:43,720
it's hard not to think of 9-11.

691
00:35:43,820 --> 00:35:46,220
We've always been involved in the terrorist activity

692
00:35:46,320 --> 00:35:47,460
even before that.

693
00:35:47,560 --> 00:35:50,960
But 9-11 brought a heightened security alert,

694
00:35:51,060 --> 00:35:55,000
so we are very much centered on not only who's

695
00:35:55,100 --> 00:35:57,440
the pilot of that aircraft, but now we need to know

696
00:35:57,540 --> 00:36:00,400
who the passengers of an aircraft are.

697
00:36:00,500 --> 00:36:02,740
Because of 9-11, the aircraft

698
00:36:02,741 --> 00:36:05,540
around the area known as the nation's capital region

699
00:36:05,640 --> 00:36:08,040
is monitored even closer than before.

700
00:36:08,140 --> 00:36:11,040
Any aircraft that comes into this 30 miles of airspace

701
00:36:11,140 --> 00:36:13,780
has to be talking to the FAA.

702
00:36:13,880 --> 00:36:16,680
If there is another 9-11 type of event

703
00:36:16,780 --> 00:36:19,820
coming into the NCR airspace, it is pointed out

704
00:36:19,920 --> 00:36:21,700
over that domestic events network.

705
00:36:21,800 --> 00:36:23,400
It is a telephone-based system.

706
00:36:25,900 --> 00:36:29,640
That line is used not only in the national capital region

707
00:36:29,641 --> 00:36:32,640
but the entire United States as well.

708
00:36:32,740 --> 00:36:37,080
When the VIPs move, the president moves.

709
00:36:37,180 --> 00:36:39,280
Maps can be layered onto the radar system

710
00:36:39,380 --> 00:36:41,680
to see exactly where a plane might be,

711
00:36:41,780 --> 00:36:45,220
as well as the terrain of the area that it's flying over.

712
00:36:45,320 --> 00:36:47,620
Additionally, the AMOC has top secret plans

713
00:36:47,720 --> 00:36:49,960
if something like 9-11 would ever happen again.

714
00:36:52,760 --> 00:36:55,060
As part of the Office of Air and Marine,

715
00:36:55,160 --> 00:36:57,260
AMOC can launch law enforcement aircraft

716
00:36:57,261 --> 00:36:59,800
to monitor targets of interest.

717
00:36:59,900 --> 00:37:02,400
Air and marine craft include Black Hawk helicopters

718
00:37:02,500 --> 00:37:06,100
and Citation jets, the fastest craft in the air and marine

719
00:37:06,200 --> 00:37:06,900
fleet.

720
00:37:09,500 --> 00:37:12,180
But perhaps one of the most advantageous craft AMOC

721
00:37:12,280 --> 00:37:15,540
has at its disposal doesn't require a pilot at all,

722
00:37:15,640 --> 00:37:17,040
at least not in the air.

723
00:37:17,140 --> 00:37:20,020
Where we are right now is in the ground control station.

724
00:37:20,120 --> 00:37:23,460
It's the unmanned aircraft system control station

725
00:37:23,461 --> 00:37:27,900
that we use to fly the unmanned aircraft.

726
00:37:28,000 --> 00:37:30,900
This is the Predator B, and it can fly upwards

727
00:37:31,000 --> 00:37:34,800
of 50,000 feet and can be in flight for over 12 hours

728
00:37:34,900 --> 00:37:35,800
at a time.

729
00:37:35,900 --> 00:37:37,800
It's equipped with an infrared sensor

730
00:37:37,900 --> 00:37:41,420
and the ability to send a live feed to the control station.

731
00:37:41,520 --> 00:37:43,020
We control everything from right here.

732
00:37:43,120 --> 00:37:47,120
We have a pilot that sits in the left seat, a sensor operator

733
00:37:47,220 --> 00:37:48,820
that is in the right seat.

734
00:37:48,920 --> 00:37:51,820
The sensor operator is responsible for working the

735
00:37:51,821 --> 00:37:56,620
working the IRs, slewing it to the positions that we need to go,

736
00:37:56,720 --> 00:37:59,760
and the pilots flying the aircraft.

737
00:37:59,860 --> 00:38:01,960
A technician is also present to keep an eye out

738
00:38:02,060 --> 00:38:05,460
for any mechanical problems.

739
00:38:05,560 --> 00:38:07,860
The agency continues to build its unmanned fleet

740
00:38:07,960 --> 00:38:10,580
to help secure the nation's airspace.

741
00:38:10,680 --> 00:38:13,180
But the most important question remains.

742
00:38:13,280 --> 00:38:17,520
What happens when AMOC finds a potential danger in the sky?

743
00:38:17,620 --> 00:38:19,980
We are there to meet that aircraft,

744
00:38:19,981 --> 00:38:21,780
and the security role, we will track him

745
00:38:21,880 --> 00:38:24,480
and take that aircraft to ground and interview

746
00:38:24,580 --> 00:38:26,180
that pilot appropriately.

747
00:38:26,280 --> 00:38:27,680
What's your name, sir?

748
00:38:27,780 --> 00:38:29,180
But if the pilot doesn't cooperate

749
00:38:29,280 --> 00:38:35,620
while in flight or becomes more of a threat,

750
00:38:35,720 --> 00:38:38,340
DOD muscle takes over.

751
00:38:38,440 --> 00:38:40,440
We don't have any aircraft that are armed.

752
00:38:40,540 --> 00:38:42,640
So if it comes to a terrorist activity,

753
00:38:42,740 --> 00:38:47,040
they will take care of the shooting down part of it.

754
00:38:47,140 --> 00:38:49,080
AMOC is constantly vigilant to keep

755
00:38:49,081 --> 00:38:51,420
dangers out of the skies.

756
00:38:51,520 --> 00:38:54,980
But how do researchers keep out a sharp tooth threat that lurks

757
00:38:55,080 --> 00:38:55,780
in the waters?

758
00:38:59,220 --> 00:39:02,560
In the early 1980s, the Air and Marine Operations Center

759
00:39:02,660 --> 00:39:05,800
used a number of radars atop all-terrain vehicles

760
00:39:05,900 --> 00:39:08,540
to monitor sections of the US border.

761
00:39:08,640 --> 00:39:11,540
The range of each was less than 100 miles.

762
00:39:16,580 --> 00:39:19,080
This site is one that fills many more

763
00:39:19,081 --> 00:39:20,740
with fear.

764
00:39:20,840 --> 00:39:24,680
Shark attacks on humans occur nearly 70 times a year.

765
00:39:24,780 --> 00:39:26,360
Of course, the safest way to avoid sharks

766
00:39:26,460 --> 00:39:27,760
would be to stay out of the water.

767
00:39:30,820 --> 00:39:34,160
However, these researchers don't have a choice.

768
00:39:34,260 --> 00:39:37,560
In order to study sharks, they must enter their environment.

769
00:39:37,660 --> 00:39:39,740
The safest way to do that is in one of these.

770
00:39:44,500 --> 00:39:47,480
Marine biologist Luke Tipple has been diving with sharks

771
00:39:47,481 --> 00:39:51,420
professionally since 2000 and knows the true keep out value

772
00:39:51,421 --> 00:39:53,720
of a cage.

773
00:39:53,721 --> 00:39:56,220
Well, a shark cage actually lets us get close to the animals

774
00:39:56,221 --> 00:39:59,160
and observe their behavior without worrying about how

775
00:39:59,161 --> 00:40:01,660
their behavior might affect us adversely.

776
00:40:01,661 --> 00:40:04,540
The cage is designed so that our divers can stay inside

777
00:40:04,541 --> 00:40:07,040
and the sharks can stay outside, but it's completely useless

778
00:40:07,041 --> 00:40:08,240
if we can't see them.

779
00:40:08,241 --> 00:40:11,880
So we built in the aperture at a height where the sharks usually

780
00:40:11,881 --> 00:40:13,080
stay around this level.

781
00:40:13,081 --> 00:40:15,880
But if they do come down, the window is small enough,

782
00:40:15,881 --> 00:40:17,580
bang into it and usually turn away.

783
00:40:17,680 --> 00:40:19,820
It's very rare that a shark might get stuck or go

784
00:40:19,920 --> 00:40:21,920
through one of these windows.

785
00:40:22,020 --> 00:40:25,620
In fact, the cage itself is a visual deterrent to sharks.

786
00:40:25,720 --> 00:40:27,360
The sharks actually have very good eyesight,

787
00:40:27,460 --> 00:40:28,820
so they can see the cage.

788
00:40:28,920 --> 00:40:31,060
It's not something that they want to charge into and bang

789
00:40:31,160 --> 00:40:31,960
into.

790
00:40:32,060 --> 00:40:33,200
They can certainly also sense it.

791
00:40:33,300 --> 00:40:35,300
It gives off an electrical signal in the water.

792
00:40:35,400 --> 00:40:37,960
So the sharks will be curious about it,

793
00:40:38,060 --> 00:40:39,540
but it's not something that they actually want to bite

794
00:40:39,640 --> 00:40:41,440
or feel endangered by.

795
00:40:41,540 --> 00:40:44,940
It would take a lot for a shark to actually bite onto a cage.

796
00:40:44,941 --> 00:40:48,840
But if it did accidentally or intentionally bite onto the cage,

797
00:40:48,940 --> 00:40:51,080
a shark's bite actually isn't all that strong.

798
00:40:51,180 --> 00:40:52,780
It's yet to be measured very well,

799
00:40:52,880 --> 00:40:54,180
but it's really not strong enough

800
00:40:54,280 --> 00:40:55,580
to bite through the aluminum.

801
00:40:55,680 --> 00:40:56,880
It would probably just bite it.

802
00:40:56,980 --> 00:41:00,280
It'd feel something wasn't right about it and let it go.

803
00:41:00,380 --> 00:41:03,300
Sharks don't usually bite cages.

804
00:41:03,400 --> 00:41:06,400
Misuse of bait like dead fish and blood, called chum

805
00:41:06,500 --> 00:41:11,600
by amateur divers, is often responsible for such incidents.

806
00:41:11,700 --> 00:41:14,400
But even if cages don't look tasty to sharks,

807
00:41:14,401 --> 00:41:17,060
they must still be specially designed and constructed.

808
00:41:20,900 --> 00:41:23,740
This is marine grade aluminum, and we'll be using

809
00:41:23,840 --> 00:41:27,340
like a marine grade aluminum TIG rod too.

810
00:41:27,440 --> 00:41:30,640
With the shark cages, usually everything we do is TIG welded.

811
00:41:30,740 --> 00:41:34,260
We get a better penetration, stronger weld,

812
00:41:34,360 --> 00:41:37,160
a lot better for high strength to try and keep the sharks

813
00:41:37,260 --> 00:41:40,300
out of the cage pretty much and keep them from breaking.

814
00:41:40,400 --> 00:41:42,060
And each cage is built for the sharks

815
00:41:42,061 --> 00:41:45,800
that inhabit the area in which it will be used.

816
00:41:45,900 --> 00:41:48,000
We usually run like a 12-inch spacing

817
00:41:48,100 --> 00:41:51,260
from center to center to try and keep the sharks out.

818
00:41:51,360 --> 00:41:52,700
If they're dealing with smaller sharks,

819
00:41:52,800 --> 00:41:54,900
we've done smaller spacing on our centers.

820
00:41:55,000 --> 00:41:57,680
This cage is going in great white territory,

821
00:41:57,780 --> 00:42:00,020
so realistically, 12 inches on center

822
00:42:00,120 --> 00:42:03,420
is more than adequate for the size of the sharks.

823
00:42:03,520 --> 00:42:05,320
Aluminum is a good choice for the cages

824
00:42:05,420 --> 00:42:08,320
because it won't corrode quickly in the water.

825
00:42:08,420 --> 00:42:10,460
Steel and iron will.

826
00:42:10,461 --> 00:42:14,160
When complete, this cage will weigh about 400 pounds.

827
00:42:14,260 --> 00:42:16,260
Researchers can also use the viewing windows

828
00:42:16,360 --> 00:42:18,260
for gathering samples.

829
00:42:18,360 --> 00:42:21,100
Just be careful where you stick your arms.

830
00:42:21,200 --> 00:42:23,200
The most efficient way to get any type of sample

831
00:42:23,300 --> 00:42:25,700
from the sharks when researchers are underwater

832
00:42:25,800 --> 00:42:28,620
is actually to just go straight through these bars.

833
00:42:28,720 --> 00:42:31,980
We can reach out and use a spear gun or a spear tip,

834
00:42:32,080 --> 00:42:33,880
and we can take small biopsies from the sharks.

835
00:42:33,980 --> 00:42:36,660
We can also tag them.

836
00:42:36,760 --> 00:42:39,580
But what if you don't have the luxury of a cage?

837
00:42:39,581 --> 00:42:41,120
There are some other deterrents out there

838
00:42:41,121 --> 00:42:43,120
to keep swimmers and divers safe.

839
00:42:43,121 --> 00:42:44,820
Your everyday swimmer really doesn't

840
00:42:44,821 --> 00:42:46,220
need to worry about sharks.

841
00:42:46,221 --> 00:42:49,220
Yes, they're in the water, but you're really not on the menu.

842
00:42:49,221 --> 00:42:51,020
If you choose to be extra cautious,

843
00:42:51,021 --> 00:42:53,620
you could perhaps wear a shark shield or some other device

844
00:42:53,621 --> 00:42:56,940
which actively repels the sharks that might come into the general

845
00:42:56,941 --> 00:42:59,340
area that you're swimming in.

846
00:42:59,341 --> 00:43:01,940
The shark shield emits an electrical field,

847
00:43:01,941 --> 00:43:05,080
essentially creating a protective bubble around the swimmer.

848
00:43:05,081 --> 00:43:07,380
The range is over 20 feet.

849
00:43:07,381 --> 00:43:09,720
Once a shark is close enough to sense that bubble,

850
00:43:09,820 --> 00:43:11,320
it will keep out.

851
00:43:11,420 --> 00:43:14,120
But not all deterrents have the desired result.

852
00:43:14,220 --> 00:43:16,160
The effectiveness of some of the shark repellents

853
00:43:16,260 --> 00:43:18,120
on the market is a little bit dubious.

854
00:43:18,220 --> 00:43:20,500
Some, like a shark repelling magnet, perhaps,

855
00:43:20,600 --> 00:43:22,000
might work in test scenarios.

856
00:43:22,100 --> 00:43:24,360
But in the open ocean, just worn on the wrist,

857
00:43:24,460 --> 00:43:26,460
it's probably not going to repel a big shark.

858
00:43:26,560 --> 00:43:30,900
In fact, last year, I fed one of these to a great white shark.

859
00:43:31,000 --> 00:43:33,080
There's also the low-tech way to keep a shark

860
00:43:33,180 --> 00:43:34,400
out of your space.

861
00:43:34,500 --> 00:43:36,400
Sometimes the least technical piece of equipment

862
00:43:36,401 --> 00:43:37,900
is often the most effective.

863
00:43:38,000 --> 00:43:40,740
This pokey stick is actually a very trusted implement

864
00:43:40,840 --> 00:43:41,640
for me.

865
00:43:41,740 --> 00:43:43,400
Whether we're tagging or taking photos,

866
00:43:43,500 --> 00:43:45,940
they can come just a little too close for comfort.

867
00:43:46,040 --> 00:43:48,840
Actually, frequently, I've had to use a stick just to tap

868
00:43:48,940 --> 00:43:51,540
them on the nose to keep them at bay.

869
00:43:51,640 --> 00:43:54,780
So the shark may not be able to read signs like us.

870
00:43:54,880 --> 00:43:57,780
But whether it's a blunt stick, an electrical field,

871
00:43:57,880 --> 00:44:01,400
or a metal cage, he still gets the message.

872
00:44:01,500 --> 00:44:04,800
And that's Keep Out.

